When compiling with gcc, higher optimization levels like -O1 can expose more errors (often called "bugs") in your code that are hidden when using no optimization (-O0). This happens because the optimizer makes assumptions about program behavior that are only valid if your code adheres to the C/C++ language standard, especially regarding undefined behavior. 
